    "turn_text": "We're not trying to decimate the Soviet Union or Russia's, because I might as well say the Soviet Union, because that's other acting, but we're not trying to decimate Russia's army. That wasn't the goal. We're just trying to put them back in the hole, back in the box they came from, get out of Ukraine. What is victory look like is basically what you're asking. As far as the way Russia's fighting the war, they're far from being out of this war. I mean, the way they fight wars is just with their fight is how they're fighting. They've never had a regard for human life. That's not what a dictatorship is. They don't care about the individual. They'll send wave after wave after wave individuals. That's how they've always fought wars, and that's how they're going to continue to fight wars. Human human life, the collateral damage means nothing, zero zilch to them. So it's what their industrial complex looks like. Their industrial complex is very strong still yet in Russia and their economy is still strong. So what does victory look like? Well, victory looks like some people in Crimea, pushing Russia all the way back into Crimea. But remember, Crimea is part of Ukraine. Russia invaded Ukraine in 2014 and stopped where they're at right now. And so Crimea is technically a legal land taken by Putin. And from 2013 to 2014, we had, remember, Obama in office at time. And he was not very happy at the fact that Trump could possibly win and he didn't want to go any farther and test it. So he stopped. If Trump wouldn't have won, they would have already done this a long time ago. So victories, some people say victory, especially Republicans say victory should just push him back out of where they're at, put him back to the line of 2014. If you're Ukraine, though, you're going to hold on a second. I'm going to fight this war. If I'm going to fight this war, then I want to make sure that we get them completely out of Ukraine. So we would support them all the way to the point where they claim victory or until they reach their own border with Russia. The minute they go into Russia, we'll be done. That's not our responsibility anymore. Our responsibility is to help them push back and invading force specifically in Russia. And so that's where we would say we're done now. I do want to hit some numbers for your listeners. Once again, I know we hit the border, but I want to hit it one more time. Just for listeners to understand what type of numbers we're dealing with on the southern border. During the Obama administration, when they claimed we had a border crisis, they said we had a crisis. Their own border agent, or not border agent, their own chief of the border at the time said that they had a crisis. And at that time, they were claiming 21,000 asylum seekers a year. a year. From 2010 to 2014, 2015, we averaged 21,000 asylum seekers a year. We're doing that now in the Biden administration every other day. Every other day. So this is how big of an issue the border is. That's why we're trying to negotiate this and why we're not saying that we know we can't let it stand by itself. And that's why we're negotiating on a greater package.",
